;
WELCOME TO HUMONGOUS RCP/M

A - Welcome to RCP/M
B - Calling the system
C - Your first visit

:A
WELCOME TO RCP/M

If you've spent any time exploring old computers, you may have wondered what
it was like to call one over the telephone. In the days before the World Wide
Web, much of the business of exchanging computer software and talking to other
enthusiasts was conducted on bulletin boards. An RCP/M was a particularly
interesting kind of board, because the caller could use the computer itself.

RCP/M stands for Remote CP/M. On HUMONGOUS RCP/M you can browse disks, read
documents, download programs, play games and exchange messages. The command
prompt belongs to a real CP/M 3 operating system, running on a Z80 emulated by
our customized version of cpmsim from Udo Munk's z80pack project. The enhanced
command environment is Z3PLUS; remote operation is handled by an adapted
version of BYE510.

We've also written a few things especially for this system. MOUNT exchanges
library disks, NDSCAN refreshes their directory names and AUTH allows the
sysop to decide which files and programs are available to callers. You needn't
concern yourself with administering AUTH to use the system.

The major difference from calling an RCP/M forty years ago is that you don't
need a modem or telephone line. Your terminal makes a network connection
instead. Otherwise, much of the experience will be familiar to anyone who used
such a system in the 1980s.

If you're looking for a particular piece of software, the library is a good
place to start. If you've come for company, try the bulletin board. And if you
simply want to spend an afternoon playing Adventure, you're welcome to do that
too.

:B
CALLING THE SYSTEM

You'll need a terminal program capable of a raw TCP connection. SyncTERM is a
good choice, especially if you intend to exchange files, since it handles the
terminal display and traditional modem transfer protocols.

Ask the sysop for the address and port, or obtain them from the board's
announcement. Choose Raw TCP, not Telnet or SSH. Use an 80-column, 24-row ANSI
or VT100-compatible terminal if possible. This is particularly important for
Maze Chase and the split-screen CHAT display.

If you're trying a copy of RCP/M on your own computer, the standard native
installation listens on:

  Setting - Value
  Connection - Raw TCP
  Address - 127.0.0.1
  Port - 4000
  Terminal - ANSI/VT100, 80 x 24

Use the address and port advertised by the sysop for a remote system; the
local address above won't reach a board elsewhere.

Connect and press RETURN. BYE should greet you with the board's welcome
message and bring you to CP/M. There may be a short delay. If the board is
already occupied, remember that this is a single-caller machine: try again
later.

The connection is not encrypted. Use a password for the bulletin board that
you do not use for anything important elsewhere.

:C
YOUR FIRST VISIT

You don't need a bulletin board account just to explore the CP/M facilities.
The board has its own registration procedure when you enter RBBS.

Here are a few things worth trying on your first call:

    HELP
    DIR
    DIR SIGM:
    MOUNT /L
    RBBS

HELP lists the available topics; DIR shows files; the SIGM directory contains
one of the large historical software collections; MOUNT /L lists the library
disks that can be selected. RBBS takes you into the message board.

Once you're in RBBS, C returns you to CP/M. Type BYE when you're ready to
finish your call. If all you've done is look around, there's nothing that
needs saving.

The rest of this guide describes these facilities in more detail. You needn't
read it from cover to cover before using the board.
